KNOWN GAP, owned by `make-a-posix-target-is-read-where-it-is-written`.

GNU Make evaluates a conditional as it reads, so `.POSIX:` in the branch that
wins is read before the fold below it. This read buffers both branches and the
evaluation chooses between them afterwards, so the flag turns on too late for a
line already read. The evaluator sets it for whatever is read after that.

GNU Make 4.4.1 keeps the blanks in front of the fold and writes `[a    b]`;
Ronin writes `[a b]`.
